Notes on particular members
---------------------------

**The raster.** `ImgData` and its row strings are global, so that a mark
made inside a `save` is still on the page after the restore that ends it,
which is what PLRM 3.7.3 requires of raster memory. They hang from the
device instance dictionary, which is a root, so they are reachable while
the device is installed and become garbage when `setpagedevice` replaces it
or the job ends.

**The two private namespaces.** `.xpostsys` is global and `privatedict` is
local, and the split is forced rather than stylistic: `privatedict` holds
local objects -- the device classes, the graphics scratch -- which a global
dictionary may not hold. `.xpostsys` is recorded in the context, which
roots it directly and gives C a route to it. `.internaldict` is global and
is reached through the references frozen into procedure bodies, and through
the `internaldict` operator, whose own procedure holds one such reference.
Both namespaces drop their `userdict` anchor when they are sealed.

**Names.** `addname` interns into the bank the allocation mode selects, so
a name first seen while allocation was global lives in global virtual
memory and one first seen while it was local lives in local. Both name
stacks are therefore populated in an ordinary run. A name object carries
the index of its characters on the stack of its own bank, and the stack
itself is a special entity, below `start`, so a sweep never takes it.

**The operator table.** An operator that runs a procedure holds that
procedure in the table, which is a special entity and is not walked by the
collector. The procedures are also kept in `.wrappedprocs`, which is
walked, and that is what keeps them from being taken for garbage.

**The font face cache.** A face is cached so that every font dictionary a
name produces shares one synthesized `CharStrings` dictionary and one
`sfnts` array. The cache is split: a C structure keeps the face the font
library opened and the file it came from, and the objects live in
`.xpostsys /.facecache`, one dictionary per face. The dictionary is made
while the boot files load, because the namespace is sealed read-only
afterwards and a member added later would be refused; the seal is
shallow, so writing into it goes on working.

Reachability outside virtual memory
-----------------------------------

An object held in a variable or a structure of C is reached by nothing
the collector walks, so the first collection takes it and the holder goes
on using whatever the storage has become. Such a holder must name its
object somewhere the walk does reach. There are three ways, and
`tests/c_held_objects.register` records which each holder uses: the
context holds it and the root list above covers it; an entity the walk
covers names it, as a file entity names the procedure a procedure-source
stream reads from; or it is reachable from an object already covered.

The register is checked by `tests/check-c-held-objects.sh`, which fails
on a holder that is not in it. The collector cannot be taught to look in
a variable it has never heard of, so nothing else would catch one.

The converse case is a holder with nothing to register. A device's
C-level instance state is a block outside virtual memory, reached through
the handle its instance dictionary carries under `/Private`, so it is
exempt from `restore` the way raster memory is (PLRM 3.7.3). A recording
device's marks are held there: the record holds no `Xpost_Object` at all,
so there is nothing for the walk to reach and nothing to register, and
past a threshold its bytes are in an unnamed scratch file and are not in
the process's memory either. So `restore` does not reach a record and
`vmstatus` does not count it, however large the drawing it holds. What
the walk does reach is the device instance dictionary, and the record
goes when that device is retired and its `Destroy` runs.

The rasters of the compiled devices that keep a buffer of their own --
png, jpeg, raster and BGR -- are outside virtual memory for the same
reason and are likewise uncounted; the raster classes' `ImgData` rows,
above, are the ones `vmstatus` sees.

Keys that outlive a restore
---------------------------

The section above asks whether the collector can still find a thing. This
one asks whether a key still names what it named. They are different
questions: naming an object to the collector keeps it from being swept
and says nothing about `restore`, which winds local virtual memory back
whether or not the walk could reach it.

Several things here are held against a number that names them. The rule
those arrangements keep is:

    No part of a key may live in storage more durable than the counter
    that mints it.

Three places matter, and they are easy to conflate because two of them
are usually the same:

  the mint    where the number comes from.
  the stamp   where it is written so the thing can be recognised again.
              This is often the program's own dictionary -- a font
              dictionary, a form dictionary -- and the bank of that
              dictionary is the program's choice, not this code's.
  the store   where whatever is filed under the number lives.

Durability runs in three steps. A value in local virtual memory is wound
back by `restore`. A value in global virtual memory is not (PLRM 3.7.3).
A value in a C variable, or bytes already written to a file, is not
reached by any winding back at all.

Break the rule and the counter goes back below a number that is still
being carried somewhere more durable. The next thing minted is issued
that number, two things are filed under one key, and whichever arrived
first answers for both. Nothing raises. A glyph is drawn from another
font's mask, a form paints another form's marks, a fill is cut by another
clip's shape. It is only ever visible as the wrong thing on a page, which
is why each of these is driven by a test that counts rather than one that
asks whether an error was raised: a cache that is never consulted raises
none either.

Two arrangements satisfy the rule, and a third sidesteps it:

  1. Mint outside virtual memory. The counter only moves forward, so no
     number is issued twice and the stamp and the store may then live
     anywhere. This is what the glyph masks, the form drawings and the
     resolved regions do.
  2. Mint, stamp and store all in one bank, so a restore takes all three
     back together and a reissued number finds nothing filed under it.
     Sound, but only while all three stay there -- and the stamp is the
     one that tends to escape, because it is written into a dictionary
     the program supplied.
  3. Mint nothing. A key derived from the content, checked against a
     stored copy of that content, cannot be reissued because it was never
     issued.

What is held, and where each part lives:

  glyph masks           mint `.newfontserial` (C); stamp `.fontid` in the
                        font dictionary, either bank; store in C, outside
                        virtual memory. The selector half of the key is a
                        name's stack index together with the bank bit,
                        since a name is that pair and nothing narrower
                        tells two of them apart.
                        tests/font_serial_test.ps

  form drawings         mint `.newformserial` (C); stamp `.formid` in the
                        form dictionary, either bank; store `.formcache`
                        in `graphicsdict`, local. The store is wound back
                        by a restore, which costs a re-capture and never
                        a wrong drawing, because the serial an entry was
                        filed under is not issued again.
                        tests/form_serial_restore_test.ps

  resolved regions      mint `.newregionserial` (C); stamp `serial` in the
  read-back clip bands  clip cache dictionary, local; store in C. The
                        entity the clip array occupied is compared as
                        well, so a restarted counter cannot match an entry
                        built from something else.

  clipPath identities   mint a counter in global virtual memory, one per
                        device; no stamp; store is the document, whose
                        written bytes cannot be taken back.
                        tests/run-svg-clipid-test.sh

  faces                 no mint: the key is the face name. The host half
                        is in C and the object half is in a dictionary
                        under the global private namespace, so both halves
                        are equally durable.

  stencil runs          no mint: the key is a content hash, and a match is
                        confirmed against a stored copy of the mask, so a
                        collision answers no differently than a miss.

A counter that runs its range restarts below numbers it has already
handed out, at which point what is held can no longer be told apart from
what the reissued numbers will name, and it has to be given up.
`.newfontserial` clears the mask cache and `.newregionserial` flushes the
region memo, both of which they can reach. `.newformserial` cannot reach
its cache, which is in virtual memory, so it restarts and the caller
notices the number is no longer greater than the last one it saw and
drops the cache itself; the number last seen is kept beside the cache so
that a restore takes both back together and the test stays true to what
the cache actually holds.

Which bank a collection may sweep
---------------------------------

A bank may only be swept by a collection that marked it. The roots above
reach an object in the other bank only when the collection is asked for
both, so sweeping a bank this walk did not cover would take storage that
is still named.

A collection that runs of its own accord is asked for both, because
marking has to cross them whatever is reclaimed and the sweep of the
second is a walk of its entity table and nothing more. `vmreclaim`
(PLRM 8.2) is the program's say in it: the negative operands turn the
automatic collection off for one bank or for both and zero turns it on
again, while 1 and 2 perform an immediate collection of local or of
both.
